  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 156 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nModels are slowing down the incoming system with each run. They now \ndepict the low digging farther south over the desert southwest \nbefore ejecting across the southern Plains. This allows the ridge of \nhigh pressure to remain over southern Missouri longer, staving off \nprecipitation chances and warming things up a bit above normal. \nWe're looking at highs in the 80s Tuesday, with low 80s more \nplausible in the low-lying areas of south-central Missouri and mid \nto upper 80s possible along the Plateau and higher elevations along \nthe Arkansas border. With the later timing of the system, Tuesday \nnow looks dry under the direct impact of the ridge apex.\nWednesday is looking like the warmest day of the week, with\nhighs soaring into the upper 80s. This will be about ten degrees\nhigher than normal, and only a few degrees shy of records.\nTuesday and Wednesday will feel quite a bit hotter than the\ntemperatures we have had lately.\n\nThere are still minor timing differences among the models on the \narrival of the system. Early models bring the initial front to \neastern Arkansas Wednesday morning while other models hold off until \nWednesday night. The deterministic NBM favors the early solution, \nbringing PoPs up to 30% along and west of Hwy 65 by midday \nWednesday, moving east through the area and bringing 50-80% PoPs by \nThursday morning. Roughly Hwy 65 and west is included in a Slight \nrisk for excessive rainfall from the WPC, with a moderate risk for \nthe remainder of the CWA. The entire area is covered by a Marginal \nERO for Thursday.\n\nThe plume of Gulf moisture that this system pushes into the Ozarks \nis truly impressive. The max PW value recorded for SGF on Sep 30 is \n1.71, and some portions of the area could have PW values up to 2.25 \ninches Wednesday, especially in extreme southwest Missouri. The \nNAEFS indicate that the forecast values are over 3 stdev above \naverage.\n\nThere is also considerable disagreement in the progressiveness of \nthe system. Comparing the GFS and the ECMWF, for example, the GFS \ndepicts rain coming to an end Friday night as moisture is \nsufficiently shoved off to the south. The ECMWF, on the other hand, \nshows the moisture plume remaining up into the southern Plains with \nsupportive low pressure behind it (remnant Polo energy?). The NBM \ndoes agree with remnant rain lasting through Friday night and \nSaturday, though with much lower probabilities. There are a lot of \nquestions regarding how the incoming low will interact with Polo, \nhow they behave as they eject out over the Plains, and how their \nabsorption into the midlatitude jet stream will affect their impact \nto our area. Keep up with forecasts through this week to get the \nlatest on this system's behavior.\n\nBelow are the NBM probabilities for at least x inches over the \n72 hours ending 7am Saturday (east-west):\n\n1\": 60-80%\n2\": 50-70%\n3\": 20-50%",
